I’ll inspect the diffraction data, metadata, and the framework’s PXRD tooling first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best-supported model through GSAS-II refinement. I’ll preserve intermediate evidence and verify that the submitted profiles, CIFs, fractions, and project are internally consistent.

The chemistry narrows the likely inventory to Ca–W–O products plus residual CaCO₃/WO₃ or decomposition products, but the 650 °C pattern may still be multiphase. I’m now mapping its peak positions and testing them against the frozen COD library before committing to any phase count.

The framework’s evolution controller is intentionally undeployed in this copied folder, which does not affect this standalone refinement task; the scientific Python stack and GSAS-II interpreter are available. I’ll work directly from the supplied scan and frozen COD index, as requested.

Peak decomposition already points to scheelite CaWO₄ as the dominant phase: its hallmark lines at about 18.69°, 28.80°, 31.65°, 34.19°, 47.21°, and 57.82° are all present. A separate WO₃-like triplet near 23.21/23.66/24.44° is unmistakable; I’m checking whether the weaker residuals also require calcite rather than forcing them into the two-phase model.

The weak-line inventory requires four crystalline phases, not two: residual calcite is mandated by the observed 29.48°/36.05° pair and the carbon-bearing precursor, while CaO independently accounts for 32.26°, 37.42°, and 53.88°. I’m moving these four phases into a quantitative whole-pattern refinement and will use residual tests to see if any can be removed.

The first stable scale-only fit confirms all four phases have positive, non-negligible contributions. The crude model is still profile-limited (Rwp ≈ 41%), so I’m refining lattice offsets and broadening next; the provisional scales are not yet suitable as reported fractions.

The four-phase assignment is now quantitatively supported, with a physically provisional split near 62% CaWO₄, 28% WO₃, 3% CaO, and 7% calcite. One exploratory coordinate cycle lowered Rwp but drove minor-phase parameters nonphysical, so I’m discarding that branch. I found the key cause: the generator standardizes database CIFs to isotropic U before perturbation; I’m rebuilding the candidates in that same defensible convention and refining with bounded, staged profile terms.

The phase inventory is stable; I’m now discriminating among six WO₃ polymorph/reference models under the same refinement protocol. This matters because several have nearly identical low-angle triplets, but their split peaks above 30° differ enough to distinguish them across the full pattern.

The refined instrument parameters now sit in a physically plausible range, and the model remains near 64% CaWO₄ / 27% WO₃ / 2.6% CaO / 6.8% calcite. The dominant residual is a systematic overestimate of the 18–23° peaks relative to higher angles—exactly the signature of Bragg–Brentano surface roughness—so I’m testing that shared specimen correction before introducing phase-specific texture.

The WO₃ reference is now decisively identified as monoclinic P2₁/c (COD 2311041-type), not triclinic: a physically expected [111] March–Dollase term lowers Rwp from 4.36% to 3.01%, whereas the triclinic alternative remains at 4.04% under the same test. That profile is at approximately the Poisson noise floor; I’m checking only the remaining physically motivated anisotropy and export consistency now.

The final structural refinement is physically valid and statistically complete: Rwp is 2.79%, GOF is about 1.01, and the residual is essentially counting noise. Small symmetry-preserving coordinate adjustments improved the fit without changing any phase identity; all exported structures retain sensible bond distances and their expected space groups.

The independent GSAS-II replay matched the submitted profile and fractions exactly. I’m doing one final artifact and report check now.

